rse_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={-1,1}$ and $B={u,v},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(v,v,1)$\n- $((v,v),1)$\n- $(1,v,u)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${u,v}$ with ${u,v}$ and ${-1,1},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\times B\\times A={ & (u,u,-1),(u,u,1),(u,v,-1),(u,v,1), \\\\  & (v,u,-1),(v,u,1),(v,v,-1),(v,v,1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|B\\times B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(v,v,1)\\in B\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44a9f5f87dfc93935f604664377abdb446e1f5a533f69f4c9d7e207150b5d657" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={0,1,2}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\n- $(1,y,2)$\n- $((1,y),2)$\n- $((y,1),y)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44a9f5f87dfc93935f604664377abdb446e1f5a533f69f4c9d7e207150b5d657" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/state.json" "file_sha256" "4353cd80f75d0977eb667ddec9022bb1b062516f5ecfd4a15235ed3a42c7f256" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Construct a schematic of the set $A^2$ for $A = \\{0,3 \\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13160.json" "file_sha256" "a8c8462cb648875705d2dc06d0b6945b08f94ccbc3448a76baef30fa4d817c31" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A^2 = A \\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/4646235c2009dc45d7d09a52162ec0bdf929d122f450791ae9917b3ad8ef5a43.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/8c9647361885913d2534fefb49a878c2e365cf5be36d1bb4c059d95344dd1a60.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13160.json" "file_sha256" "a8c8462cb648875705d2dc06d0b6945b08f94ccbc3448a76baef30fa4d817c31" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the sets $A = \\{3,4,7\\},$ $B = \\{f,g\\},$ and $C = A \\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(f,4) \\in C$\n\n- $(7,g) \\in C$\n\n- $(3,f) \\in C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13151.json" "file_sha256" "11908d28785e51baba7b1e8379148dc62af3adb8d2909e120d673cd442bbac67" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/683bad9c822cea7218b2fd8a252e46215103c95b3ee9c42e41e7141ed8a40330.png)\n\nHence, the set $C = A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{ \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13151.json" "file_sha256" "11908d28785e51baba7b1e8379148dc62af3adb8d2909e120d673cd442bbac67" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $C = \\{ 3, 4, 7, 9, 11 \\}$ and $D  =\\{ 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 10 \\},$ what is the cardinality of $C \\times D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13154.json" "file_sha256" "ee1eaea891c69fddc586c269acbcd4b2633f118b5f03d5f1f59d85152ca0ba22" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D,$ the cardinality of $C \\times D$ is\n\n$$\n| C \\times D | = |C| \\cdot |D|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|C\\times D| & =|C|\\cdot |D| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13154.json" "file_sha256" "ee1eaea891c69fddc586c269acbcd4b2633f118b5f03d5f1f59d85152ca0ba22" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A = \\{ p,q \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4,9 \\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A \\times B) \\times B?$\n\n- $((p,4),9)$\n\n- $((q,9),4)$\n\n- $(p,4,9)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13152.json" "file_sha256" "5f5fb79636b1e812fb7fe02cf2c93b67f4d6cc3d424b4bf22b3806c67459e738" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{ p,q \\}$ with $\\{ 4,9 \\},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/69ff8391a11b7bfa221b6b303f31551288bd969bdeed778fb05d3b7094e027ae.png)\n\nHence, the set $A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\{ (p,4), (p,9), (q,4), (q,9)\\}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A \\times B| = |A| \\cdot |B| = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A \\times B) \\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A \\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/5f4a74a98f9df15273d59131eadc1155ff2d1105e1027e7d10b7e2366102e046.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A \\times B) \\times B| = |A \\times B| \\cdot |B| = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((p, 4), 9)}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((q,9),4)}$ are in $(A \\times B) \\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/example-13152.json" "file_sha256" "5f5fb79636b1e812fb7fe02cf2c93b67f4d6cc3d424b4bf22b3806c67459e738" "basis" 1434 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-181957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times B$ for $A={-1,1}$ and $B={x,y,z}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/8fe661f4daa7a5bb75dea993f46bbf8970400a798e23da4018d3e4a0b891273d.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/49270545404b21e8068b35cdb59c756e1f3bc3c22608359c8d7e83389e38c7a4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/1afafe8de19ab9a48d7be7cbe8ff66840008b642595a731c58e687e96cef49f7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/5e14edf4c5890bc213d9a488b9135e142a369ea6ee0259cc183a523b7c5316e4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/63d6365a037294d0ceb4f320b86b16ce2c70011a8c5660ba7cbb283179026091.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/6aee614cc6259f0f9d84d25764a325320faa55419f848943dfd92dca50160cd1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/88fe1481ff4c18a96f93d452bf8624851049c1b378504728a94eaac354321a9d.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/5e14edf4c5890bc213d9a488b9135e142a369ea6ee0259cc183a523b7c5316e4.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/bf1c37a92b184cb3e6c81bbd2d653008f5eff0e3b2131f2f297a336881661c22.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={p,q,r},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/919ad6e16dcd1d8f02e8c15c7bd1dfa15f67232c99706dfbc1239e1f9a0f99e5.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(q,q)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- $(p,r)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- ${q}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/d6ee87bd861491394c6643679f62cb4b94436bf2777bd2436b3169e779736e2c.png)\n\nUsing the visual schematic above for the Cartesian product ${A}^{2}$ for $A={h,k},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}$\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The set ${A}^{2}$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A\\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times A,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set $(A)$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set $(A)$ at each intersection.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/507f8f6306c309d5e894cfb1dabfc2019f371911f7878b196e1f329f1b37dfa6.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- ${h}^{2}\\in {A}^{2}\\,\\times $\n\n- $(h,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\n- $(k,k)\\in {A}^{2}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times B$ for $A={p,q}$ and $B={w,z}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/6baae52e45d00d31eac732a23be655b648765c27cfcdc667c511b176d5bbbc88.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/38e9d12b0c09ae066149d95235a6918712214edc147c57775da2d880b584cb9e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/518415814ddb5483e4649c95e58aa41adc68ad8722279dbf99ac4c10bca206e9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/6cceec57dbf53e768fc290d864fa770c70a6642bdc9df1309119e113241b0f34.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/a52e05a3884e5d62ffaacc73104a4f2e61fb8013fb353c850cdb974e66224a40.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/b7497f60cccce44a008c6550f356d32564c2a15e23d88f9c3764415598dc493b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/d366ec9f363cf38d84c6d741491f7e218e0bedad335f6fc1f1f954f95516f196.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/518415814ddb5483e4649c95e58aa41adc68ad8722279dbf99ac4c10bca206e9.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/cb3e6ef6a832f2adbbc5e096bddf2c4f6a0445fbd77a71df5617298f8dad2e51.png)\n\nBy finding the missing entries in the schematic shown above for $A\\times B,$ where $A={a,b,c}$ and $B={2,4,6},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(4,b)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times B,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B$ at each intersection.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/57d053cca53046f02201540e655030a37ba8f7a2811e7dc724cf30ca9fd3c072.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(4,b)\\notin A\\times B\\,\\times $\n\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181963" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181963" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181963" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the sets $Z={-3,6,-9}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(-3,6,-3)\\in Y$\n- $(-3,-9)\\in Y$\n- $(-9,6)\\in Y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/0c58bf1f025cc4960ce3b9dc8e1bc3bf474a521473833b27d136fd77fd6b814a.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(-3,-3),(-3,6),(-3,-9),(6,-3),(6,6),(6,-9),(-9,-3)(-9,6),(-9,-9)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-182022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182022" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the sets $X={x,z},$ $Y={a,b},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(a,x)\\in Z$\n- $(z,a)\\in Z$\n- $(z,z)\\in Z$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/b73e7aeb017bd9f7c50f7d110c7933763834eb391ac4a3e97d25f4e587a8a3d4.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,a),(x,b),(z,a),(z,b)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-182011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-1261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the sets $X={x,y,z},$ $Y={v,w},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(x,v)\\in Z$\n- $(y,w)\\in Z$\n- $(w,z)\\in Z$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-1261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/316a12102e4be81291f06403815cfb63749b1fd3f463c465294e031543123484.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,v),(x,w),(y,v),(y,w),(z,v),(z,w)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-1261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-1261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-1261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-1261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the sets $Z={t,u}$ and $Y=Z\\times Z,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(u,t)\\in Y$\n- $(u,t,u)\\in Y$\n- $({t}^{2},{u}^{2})\\in Y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $Z\\times Z$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $Z$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Z.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for ${Z}^{2}=Z\\times Z$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/fa14e54c0518f8f5e64d3ccb4f725bd0b3804fb197d7b90320398ebbf09d68d4.png)\n\nHence, the set $Y=Z\\times Z$ is given by\n\n$$\nY={(t,t),(t,u)(u,t),(u,u)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-182020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182009" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the sets $A={k,m},$ $B={-1,3,5},$ and $C=A\\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(-1,k)\\in C$\n- $(k,m)\\in C$\n- $(m,3)\\in C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182009" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A\\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/c660ff6d042bdf228387e5ad55651f02b136f738a2e4fa99c3ef3b3faef3e3c5.png)\n\nHence, the set $C=A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC={(k,-1),(k,3),(k,5),(m,-1),(m,3),(m,5)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182009" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-182009" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182009" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-182009" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the cardinality of $K\\times L$ for $K={a,b,c,d}$ and $L={0,1,2,3,…,8}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any two finite sets $K$ and $L,$ the cardinality of $K\\times L$ is\n\n$$\n|K\\times L|=|K|\\cdot |L|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|K\\times L| & =|K|\\cdot |L| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-105860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "39"] ["math" "42"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "36"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the cardinality of $M\\times M$ for $M={1,2,3…,10}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any two finite sets $A$ and $B,$ the cardinality of $A\\times B$ is\n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|M\\times M| & =|M|\\cdot |M| \\\\  & =10\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-105862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "100"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "200"] ["math" "50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "100"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the cardinality of $X\\times Z$ for $X={11,12,13,...,20}$ and $Z={1,6,8,16,24,30}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any two finite sets $X$ and $Z,$ the cardinality of $X\\times Z$ is\n\n$$\n|X\\times Z|=|X|\\cdot |Z|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|X\\times Z| & =|X|\\cdot |Z| \\\\  & =10\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-80893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "60"] ["math" "80"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the cardinality of $M\\times N$ for $M={a,b,c,d,e,f,g}$ and $N={a,b,c,d,e,f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any two finite sets $M$ and $N,$ the cardinality of $M\\times N$ is\n\n$$\n|M\\times N|=|M|\\cdot |N|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|M\\times N| & =|M|\\cdot |N| \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105861" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-105861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13"] ["math" "42"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "49"] ["math" "54"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-105861" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "42"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the cardinality of $K\\times M$ for $K={0,3,4,11,15}$ and $M={d,e,f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any two finite sets $K$ and $M,$ the cardinality of $K\\times M$ is\n\n$$\n|K\\times M|=|K|\\cdot |M|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|K\\times M| & =|K|\\cdot |M| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-80897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "45"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-80897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={p,q}$ and $B={2,4,6},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times A\\times B?$\n\n- $(p,q,2)$\n- $((p,p),4)$\n- $(q,q,6)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${p,q}$ with ${p,q}$ and ${2,4,6},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times A\\times B={ & (p,p,2),(p,p,4),(p,p,6),(p,q,2),(p,q,4),(p,q,6), \\\\  & (q,p,2),(q,p,4),(q,p,6),(q,q,2),(q,q,4),(q,q,6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=12.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(p,q,2)$ and $(q,q,6)$ are in $A\\times A\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={a,b}$ and $B={1,2},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times A\\times B?$\n\n- $(a,a,1)$\n- $(b,a,2)$\n- $((a,b),2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${a,b}$ with ${a,b}$ and ${1,2},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times A\\times B={ & (a,a,1),(a,a,2),(a,b,1),(a,b,2), \\\\  & (b,a,1),(b,a,2),(b,b,1),(b,b,2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $(a,a,1)$ and $(b,a,2)$ are in $A\\times A\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={0,1,2}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\n- $(1,y,2)$\n- $((1,y),2)$\n- $((y,1),y)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181294" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={8,9}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $A\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(9,9,y)$\n- $(8,x,y)$\n- $(9,y,8)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and the third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${8,9}$ with ${x,y}$ and ${8,9},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times B\\times A={ & (8,x,8),(8,x,9),(8,y,8),(8,y,9), \\\\  & (9,x,8),(9,x,9),(9,y,8),(9,y,9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times A|=|A|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(9,y,8)\\in A\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181942" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181942" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={-1,1}$ and $B={u,v},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(v,v,1)$\n- $((v,v),1)$\n- $(1,v,u)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${u,v}$ with ${u,v}$ and ${-1,1},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\times B\\times A={ & (u,u,-1),(u,u,1),(u,v,-1),(u,v,1), \\\\  & (v,u,-1),(v,u,1),(v,v,-1),(v,v,1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|B\\times B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(v,v,1)\\in B\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c576684a729c4b14a9eccb5cd7ef705fb0209de894412a2911c3cfa4797043"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "q-181295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/state.json" "file_sha256" "9489c02ba56b6d85f4d8183690d4656baa403cff5ec510332396254f00b5a592"} {"question" "e-13160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Construct a schematic of the set $A^2$ for $A = \\{0,3 \\}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13160.json" "file_sha256" "60829c69cf8f6bc9e5c7b0b9d1e462bdd2ad0977d3d31d0a0f34859216506da0"} {"question" "e-13160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A^2 = A \\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/4646235c2009dc45d7d09a52162ec0bdf929d122f450791ae9917b3ad8ef5a43.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/8c9647361885913d2534fefb49a878c2e365cf5be36d1bb4c059d95344dd1a60.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13160.json" "file_sha256" "60829c69cf8f6bc9e5c7b0b9d1e462bdd2ad0977d3d31d0a0f34859216506da0"} {"question" "e-13151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the sets $A = \\{3,4,7\\},$ $B = \\{f,g\\},$ and $C = A \\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(f,4) \\in C$\n\n- $(7,g) \\in C$\n\n- $(3,f) \\in C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13151.json" "file_sha256" "e862d7d06d504b78107ac2bfe720a7977af432950b37292710f8905ade527217"} {"question" "e-13151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/683bad9c822cea7218b2fd8a252e46215103c95b3ee9c42e41e7141ed8a40330.png)\n\nHence, the set $C = A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{ \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13151.json" "file_sha256" "e862d7d06d504b78107ac2bfe720a7977af432950b37292710f8905ade527217"} {"question" "e-13154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $C = \\{ 3, 4, 7, 9, 11 \\}$ and $D  =\\{ 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 10 \\},$ what is the cardinality of $C \\times D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13154.json" "file_sha256" "31ff7ea17e7dffffb960abacad5b92f786ba54cc45f22c1148d0ed309e01d2d9"} {"question" "e-13154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D,$ the cardinality of $C \\times D$ is\n\n$$\n| C \\times D | = |C| \\cdot |D|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|C\\times D| & =|C|\\cdot |D| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13154.json" "file_sha256" "31ff7ea17e7dffffb960abacad5b92f786ba54cc45f22c1148d0ed309e01d2d9"} {"question" "e-13152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A = \\{ p,q \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4,9 \\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A \\times B) \\times B?$\n\n- $((p,4),9)$\n\n- $((q,9),4)$\n\n- $(p,4,9)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13152.json" "file_sha256" "177bd645186761c74b0a0c84c98fa0304552482f16d8c79882220b42caa1ae7b"} {"question" "e-13152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{ p,q \\}$ with $\\{ 4,9 \\},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/69ff8391a11b7bfa221b6b303f31551288bd969bdeed778fb05d3b7094e027ae.png)\n\nHence, the set $A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\{ (p,4), (p,9), (q,4), (q,9)\\}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A \\times B| = |A| \\cdot |B| = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A \\times B) \\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A \\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/5f4a74a98f9df15273d59131eadc1155ff2d1105e1027e7d10b7e2366102e046.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A \\times B) \\times B| = |A \\times B| \\cdot |B| = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((p, 4), 9)}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((q,9),4)}$ are in $(A \\times B) \\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/example-13152.json" "file_sha256" "177bd645186761c74b0a0c84c98fa0304552482f16d8c79882220b42caa1ae7b"}] "usage" {"e-13151" [] "e-13154" [] "e-13152" [] "q-1261" [] "e-13160" [] "q-181963" [] "q-181295" [] "q-181294" [] "q-181957" []} "basis" 2643 "retractions" [[17592186307278 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/8fe661f4daa7a5bb75dea993f46bbf8970400a798e23da4018d3e4a0b891273d.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/49270545404b21e8068b35cdb59c756e1f3bc3c22608359c8d7e83389e38c7a4.png)"] [17592186307285 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/cb3e6ef6a832f2adbbc5e096bddf2c4f6a0445fbd77a71df5617298f8dad2e51.png)\n\nBy finding the missing entries in the schematic shown above for $A\\times B,$ where $A={a,b,c}$ and $B={2,4,6},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(4,b)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B$"] [17592186307285 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times B,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B$ at each intersection.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/57d053cca53046f02201540e655030a37ba8f7a2811e7dc724cf30ca9fd3c072.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(4,b)\\notin A\\times B\\,\\times $\n\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186307250 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/316a12102e4be81291f06403815cfb63749b1fd3f463c465294e031543123484.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,v),(x,w),(y,v),(y,w),(z,v),(z,w)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186054984 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186054351 :question/worked-solution "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A^2 = A \\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/4646235c2009dc45d7d09a52162ec0bdf929d122f450791ae9917b3ad8ef5a43.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/8c9647361885913d2534fefb49a878c2e365cf5be36d1bb4c059d95344dd1a60.png)"] [17592186054348 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/683bad9c822cea7218b2fd8a252e46215103c95b3ee9c42e41e7141ed8a40330.png)\n\nHence, the set $C = A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{ \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186054349 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{ p,q \\}$ with $\\{ 4,9 \\},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14